Subject: Deciding which warehouses to open and how to serve customers at the lowest total cost

Team,

We’re at a decision point on our distribution footprint. We have a set of candidate warehouse sites and a set of customer destinations, each defined by map coordinates. Our task is to decide which warehouses to open and how to route shipments so that every customer’s needs are met while we spend as little as possible overall. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates, so proximity on the map directly drives transport cost.

What we have on hand:
- Candidate warehouse locations with map coordinates: {FACILITY_COORDINATES}
- Customer delivery locations with map coordinates: {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}
- Fixed opening cost for each warehouse: {FIXED_COSTS}
- Maximum capacity available at each warehouse: {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}
- Demand that must be satisfied for each customer: {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}
- Variable transportation rate used to price shipments: {TRANSPORT_RATE}

Business rules and expectations:
- We may open any subset of the candidate warehouses, but a location can only ship if it is opened.
- Every customer’s demand must be fully satisfied, and no warehouse can ship more than its available capacity.
- Shipping cost scales with distance and volume; because we use Euclidean distances from the coordinates above, closer pairings reduce variable spend.

The decision we’re solving for is a cost-minimizing network design: select which warehouses to open and determine how much to ship from each open site to each customer so that we meet all demand without exceeding capacity. Total cost is the sum of the fixed costs to open the chosen sites and the distance-based transportation spend. In practice, this is a trade-off between opening more sites to be closer to customers (lower transport cost, higher fixed cost) versus operating fewer sites and shipping farther (higher transport cost, lower fixed cost). Capacity limits and the geographic pattern of demand will influence where it makes sense to open.

Deliverables from this work will include:
- The recommended set of warehouses to open, with a clear rationale linking cost, capacity, and geography.
- A shipment plan showing how customer demand is covered by the open sites and the expected total cost under that plan.
- Sensitivity considerations (e.g., how the decision might change if demand shifts or if the transport rate changes).

Please confirm that the coordinate lists, volumes, and cost inputs above are current and that using straight-line distances is an appropriate proxy for our lanes. Once confirmed, we’ll proceed to quantify the trade-offs and present a recommended configuration.
